    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "> I've been investigating a config problem on my windows + powershell system, and I think spawn() is broken right now if shellslash is true:\n> https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/d343a1e230bf61bff41a78583c97ae39c5c961f3/runtime/lua/vim/_core/system.lua#L320\n> exepath returns a path conforming to shellslash, but while Powershell can respect forward-slash paths, I don't think `CreateProcess` (used by vim.system on Windows) can.  This causes a weird failure I'm still figuring out where I can't invoke `vim.system({'cmd.exe', ...})` without getting an invalid syntax error due to path being slash-based.  This might be a pitfall for this patchset, so just watch out.\n> \n> To reproduce, you should be able to run (under Windows-native cmd or powershell):\n> \n> ```pwsh\n> nvim --headless -u NONE -c \"set shellslash\" -c \"lua print('exepath: '..vim.fn.exepath('cmd.exe')); local r=vim.system({'cmd.exe','/c','echo OK'}):wait(); print('code='..r.code..' stdout='..vim.inspect(r.stdout)..' stderr='..vim.inspect(r.stderr))\" -c \"qall!\"\n> ```\n> Instead of echoing OK, this should print the forward-slashed cmd.exe and \n> ```code=1 stdout=\"\" stderr=\"The syntax of the command is incorrect.\\r\\n\"```\n> and removing \"set shellslash\" prevents the error.\n> \n> The point is, just watch out with path slashing because in this particular case it has an effect on vim.system, which is what LLM agents use to invoke commands.  That Windows edge case should probably be sorted out if there is going to be a standardization of path slashing. \n\n _Originally posted by @jbsolomon in [#39382](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/39382#issuecomment-5026231652)_",
